ios: music renders ahead only while it is needed

A song was rendered whole as soon as it started (about 19 times real time) and all of it queued as AudioBuffers: 176 KB
a second of music, 33 MB for a median song and 50+ MB for the long ones, alive in the page for minutes (with a song
change, the old song's and the new one's together: peaks of 70-170 MB in the music harness). Now, while a song plays,
its render stops once 20 s are queued and goes on when 10 s are left: the same chunks in the same order, rendered in
bursts at the same speed, so the buffers are of the same few seconds as before; at most about 25 s of music (4.4 MB)
is held.

A new song starts on a fresh synth (the soundfont loaded again from the copy setSoundfont made, about 2 ms):
tsf_reset only ends quickly the voices that are sounding, so a song began with whatever the previous one's render had
reached, which with the render held would be the previous song's middle. Upstream reached the previous song's end
(rendered whole within seconds) unless the song changed sooner; there a new song began with the old one's notes still
sounding, from a few milliseconds to minutes. A loop (replay) keeps tsf_reset: its render has reached the song's end.

Proof (a script outside the repository, a deterministic clock and the real wasm and soundfont, by the
audible samples, cut at every stop): with the fresh synth in both, the render-ahead limit is sample-exact (7 scripted
and seeded sessions, 2.6 h of music); held music 12-49 MB on average -> 1.4-3.1, peaks 71-167 -> 6.6-9.3 MB.

